// @mode: run-pass
// @decisions: D-205, D-273
//! A binding used twice: the first call gets a new reference, the last
//! call takes the binding's own, so the line prints twice. This test
//! checks the output only: a string literal is a static object, which
//! reference counting never changes, so the leak check cannot see where
//! dups and drops go. The MIR goldens pin their placement, and
//! `cheby_jit`'s `rc_placement_frees_every_object` checks a heap object at
//! run time.

import std::io

fn main() {
  let greeting = "hello"
  io::println(greeting)
  io::println(greeting)
}
